Day 9 of my DevOps Troubleshooting Series

🔥 Real-Time DevOps Scenario: 500 Internal Server Error

Imagine users can access the application, but suddenly some requests are returning:

500 Internal Server Error

As a DevOps Engineer, I wouldn't immediately restart the application. I would first identify the actual root cause.

🔍 My Troubleshooting Approach:

1️⃣ Check Application Logs

Start by checking the application logs for the exact exception or error.

Look for:
• Application exceptions
• Database errors
• Configuration issues
• OutOfMemory errors
• Dependency failures

2️⃣ Check Recent Deployments

Find out whether the issue started after a recent deployment.

I would verify:
• Latest code changes
• Configuration changes
• Environment variables
• Dependency updates

3️⃣ Check Application Health

Even when the service is running, the application can still return 500 errors.

```bash
systemctl status <application-service>
```

4️⃣ Check Database Connectivity

Verify whether the application can communicate with the database.

```bash
nc -zv <db-host> <db-port>
```

Also check database availability, credentials, connection pool, and slow queries.

5️⃣ Check Environment Variables

A missing or incorrect environment variable can cause application requests to fail.

```bash
env | grep <VARIABLE_NAME>
```

6️⃣ Check Server Resources

```bash
top
free -m
df -h
```

Check CPU, memory, and disk utilization.

🧩 Example Root Cause

Suppose the application was working normally before a deployment.

After the deployment, users started receiving 500 errors.

The application logs show that it cannot connect to the database because the database password/configuration was incorrect.

🛠️ Resolution

✅ Identify the exact exception from logs
✅ Check recent deployment and configuration changes
✅ Correct the database configuration
✅ Restart/redeploy if required
✅ Test the application
✅ Monitor logs and metrics after recovery
✅ Document the root cause and preventive action

💡 Key Takeaway

A 500 Internal Server Error doesn't necessarily mean the server is down.

The important thing is to identify where the request is failing:

Client → Load Balancer/Nginx → Application → Database/Dependencies → Logs

As a DevOps Engineer, I believe the goal is not just to restore the application, but to understand why it failed and how to prevent it from happening again.
